how does one discover life on other  planets well the first thing you need  are other planets going around our Sun  are eight planets plus Pluto and a bunch  of moons and lots of other stuff but  it's a small selection so your best bet  is to look for planets going around any  and all stars our galaxy alone is a  hundred billion stars in it and so there  is a lot of hope of seeing an enormous  amount of planets as long as we have a  way of seeing them here are some ways  you can see planets going around other  stars the first is the transit method  the transit method is a method of seeing  a planet by watching for it block the  light of the star in other words if the  planet is reasonably big compared to the  star when it orbits the star it could  get in our way and we would see that the  light from the star dims and then  brightens again with this very specific  pattern which would be caused by a  planet transiting in front of it and in  fact at this point thousands of planets  going around other stars have been seen  using the transit method a second method  that people use is a method basically  looking for the wobble of a star a  planet like Jupiter can be quite heavy  even compared to the star such that when  it goes around the star the star wobbles  and when it wobbles towards and away  from us it shifts the frequency of light  that's coming towards us it's called a  Doppler shift and that Doppler shift  happening at a very regular pattern  period tells us that there's something  heavy going around it  a third way is if stars and planets pass  in between us and more distant bright  stars that passing can cause the light  to be warped a little bit it's called  gravitational lensing predicted by  Einstein all these methods and more our  hopes for seeing thousands tens of  thousands even hundreds of thousands of  planets in the near and mediate future  but then you want to figure out if  there's life on any of those planets how  are we gonna do that well the best bet  is to look at the atmosphere the  atmosphere will contain all kinds of  chemicals and those chemicals could have  been naturally there due to the  formation of the planet in the first  place or they could be there because  something interesting is happening on  the surface of the planet giants planets  like Jupiter and bigger naturally have  water in their atmospheres but tiny  rocky planets shouldn't and they  shouldn't because sunlight breaks apart  water and hydrogen is so light it wants  to escape light planets so look for  small rocky planets that have lots of  water in their atmosphere already that  will suggest that there is water on the  surface of the rocky planet like oceans  it doesn't prove there's life on the  planet but at least now we're getting  more evidence and we're seeing ways of  seeing planets that look more and more  like the earth a final stage would be to  look at that atmosphere and find other  things in the atmosphere with abundances  amounts that are unexpected to occur  simply naturally one of those is for  example oxygen oxygen can be something  which is produced by living organisms so  if you look at a planet and in its  atmosphere there is water and there's  large levels of oxygen and perhaps other  chemicals that we might expect to be  produced by the processes of life then  we start to have strong evidence for the  existence of life to really go the final  step you need really provable  theoretical models of what life can and  cannot do and what non-life processes  can do and put chemicals into the  atmosphere as to how are we going to be  sure we don't know other than let's say  direct communication with the  intelligent beings out there  however we do see a path to get closer  and closer to eventually answering that  question are we alone
 

